module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_required_witness_enrolled_on_an_entry_whose_closure_exceeds_a_bounded_slot_is_unenforceable

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable, Mitigatable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

// THE CLASS: a plan names a real, executable witness on an entry whose transitive closure is larger
// than any bounded acquisition can carry. The naming costs nothing at the moment it is written; the
// whole price lands on the lane that later has to run the label. Filed here rather than as a rung
// drop because no rung fell: nothing ever bounded the enrolment, so the state is new to the ledger
// and not a regression of a guarantee that used to hold (gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder).
data a_required_witness_enrolled_on_an_entry_whose_closure_exceeds_a_bounded_slot_is_unenforceable: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_required_witness_enrolled_on_an_entry_whose_closure_exceeds_a_bounded_slot_is_unenforceable" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "price an entry before a lane is asked to enter it: the capability to attach to each named claim entry the elapsed bound its transitive typecheck was measured to cost at a pinned tree on the fleet's own instrument, and to admit a witness enrolment only against a bound the current bounded acquisition can carry — so that naming a witness on an unreachable entry is refused at authoring time rather than discovered by the lane that pays for it",
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    "**a required witness is enrolled on an entry whose closure exceeds any bounded slot, so the obligation is named, real, executable in principle, and unenforceable in fact.** INVALID STATE: a plan enrols a semantic validation witness as `ValidationOperation.GunbcClaimValidation { entry: FilePath, function: NonEmptyStr }` (dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_execution_contract.dag:33-37), the `function` is a real `test fn` in a real file that would discriminate, and the `entry` named is a claim module whose import closure spans the live roadmap authority. Executing ONE label on that entry first typechecks the entry's transitive closure, and that typecheck runs longer than the longest bounded acquisition a lane can hold — so the obligation has no route that ends. Nothing refuses the enrolment: it is one record literal in a plan file, no field of `ValidationOperation`, `WorkItemExecutionContract` (dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_execution_contract.dag:83-91) or `RoadmapNode` carries an expected cost, an elapsed bound or a closure bound (grep -cE of `elapsed|budget|timeout` over that module prints 0 at origin/main da49c960ace), and the enrolment itself does not compile, run or measure anything. THE SHARP EDGE IS THE GRAIN MISMATCH: the enrolment names a (entry, function) pair, the cost is a property of the ENTRY ALONE, and picking the smallest function on the entry buys nothing — the lane pays the entry before it reaches the function.",

    "NEIGHBOURS IN THIS LEDGER, AND WHY BREAKING ONE CANNOT SEE THE OTHER. `a_claim_run_never_reaches_its_first_verdict_because_its_floor_is_a_whole_tree_closure` records the cost mechanism inside one run — a claim run stalling at `body_lowering_fold` with the whole-tree floor rising with every row anyone files. Its repair is a per-claim closure or a cached one; land that and a plan can still enrol a witness on an entry whose own import closure exceeds the slot, because this class is about the ENROLMENT, not the fold. Conversely this row's repair (a cost admission at enrolment) leaves that fold cost exactly where it is. `gunbc_run_pays_a_large_fixed_pre_entry_cost_per_invocation` records the term that does NOT scale with the entry; the specimen below measures both terms on the same host (a 7,159 B entry paying ~360 s before any selection line, and a large entry failing to finish at 1860 s), and removing the fixed term would not make the closure term fit in a slot. `witness_outside_gate_closure_falsified_by_other_file` and `an_authored_gate_reached_by_no_execution_route` are both controls that are never reached; their repairs are routing — wire the route and the class below appears immediately, because a route that exists can still be unenterable. `witness_that_fails_to_compile_is_absent_rather_than_red` polices a witness that cannot compile; here the witness compiles and the entry is the problem, which is why that row's control (build the witness) passes this class untouched. `a_red_control_that_was_never_red_on_any_tree_reads_as_a_control` polices named claims that do not discriminate; these claims DO discriminate, and no green/red reading distinguishes them from an unreachable pair.",

    "THE MEASURED SPECIMEN, by a named identity, not by this row's author. Session tidy-otter-238, host srv1, BOOT c095c0cd, instrument sha256 4a0fb559533d566a42e66726bab70751dde512710b37a62c7bf7554cd7e4d05c (built at tree ec82386d665c12fd04e1eb7d1c5d5a0608b85934; he records it MANIFEST-UNMATCHED against released manifest `gunbc` line b8c123b298c024da5a0e19674bec4e20583d1e5a99d2a0c6ffa8d85a7424905f), in one heavy-slot acquisition (his ACQ5, slot 6/12, pid 1919769, cgroup heavy-slot-1920355.scope). CONTROL LEG, the claim route over the small sibling entry `//test/claim/roadmap/roadmap_status:witness_merged_pr_is_merged` (that file is 7,159 B at origin/main): `HELD outcome=Pass eval_steps=28 cpu_ms=0` with the selection line `claim route: //test/claim/roadmap/roadmap_status:witness_merged_pr_is_merged - 1 claim(s): 1 held, 0 did not hold, 0 unobserved`, rc 0, elapsed 360 s — a fixed ~361 s paid once per process BEFORE any operand is evaluated. AUTHORITY LEG, ONE named label on the live authority entry `//test/claim/roadmap/roadmap_authority:<fn>` (that claim module is 112,619 B with 62 `test fn` labels and 34 direct imports, identical at ec82386d665c and at origin/main da49c960ace; its operand plan file dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_authority.dag is 1,744,473 B with sha256 ae764adf84a0057b6e66bb0f4e0e76ff059b3f400a4c7f09cca6318f062b1d60 at ec82386d665c — this row's author recomputed that digest with `git show ec82386d665c:dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_authority.dag | sha256sum` and it matches — against 1,744,787 B and sha256 5310e19c41564621333973d5410a470703c0ca1494fa3bc95c58dc9bff4883ed at origin/main, so the operand is pinned to that tree and is not current; ec82386d665c is not an ancestor of origin/main): `LEG_A2_RC=124 TIMEOUT_NOT_RESULT`, `LEG2_VERDICT_PRESENT=0`, output still growing at the cap — 36 completed `typecheck <module> done` lines, several of them minutes each. That is a transitive typecheck of the live authority dependency graph, NOT the pre-selection floor (the control leg is entirely floor and completed inside it) and NOT a hang (the lines keep arriving). PRIMARY CITATION: https://github.com/gunb-ai/gunbc/pull/17190#issuecomment-7224581812, his own comment carrying those numbered lines. This row deliberately does NOT re-run the 1860 s leg: the claim is a bounded non-completion already executed by a named identity, and spending an hour of a shared slot to reproduce it is waste, not diligence.",

    "WHY THE OBVIOUS CHECK DID NOT CATCH IT. The plan FILE is not the cost and its own refusal is not the cost either: the whole of dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_authority.dag passes `gunbc compile --target dag` with `0 blocking error(s), 16904 advisory diagnostic(s)` at rc 0 in 583 s, and a deliberately broken copy of it refuses in 49 s with `no readable reference set (cause=parse-failed at dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_authority.dag:6860:20: expected RParen, found Ident)` — measurements by session royal-newt-240 on srv1 with /opt/gunbc/bin/gunbc sha256 8ce1a98c7bd9f0c6b8393c2e8117dae050ebab53e2175d7545435c4ff806e9e9, labelled main-deployed, MANIFEST-UNMATCHED (release-bins.manifest is not in the repo and no named sibling matches that binary, so `manifest-matched` is not satisfiable today and is not claimed here). A clean compile of a plan says the plan parses and typechecks; it says nothing about the cost of executing a witness the plan later names, and nothing in the authoring path measures that. Nor is the ledger itself gated: grep -rl `recurring_failure_mode` over .github/workflows prints 0 files at origin/main da49c960ace, and grep for `--target dag` over the same workflows prints 0 hits — so at the moment the enrolment is written, no check prices it.",

    "HARM. The lane pays first and learns second. One label on that entry costs a full bounded acquisition and returns NO verdict at all (`LEG2_VERDICT_PRESENT=0`) — not a red, not a green, not a refusal — so the obligation stays open with no failure to repair: the witness is not broken, the claim did not fail, and the only signal is a timeout that a reader can just as easily blame on their own machine. The cost is also EXCLUSIVE: the acquisition is one of a bounded pool of host slots, so an unenterable leg does not merely delay that lane, it holds a slot other lanes need. And the state is re-authorable without limit: every further witness named on the same entry inherits the same unreachable cost, invisibly, and each re-attempt by a different lane spends another bounded acquisition on the same non-completion.",

    "THE INTERIM PRE-CHECK, NAMED AS FLEET PRACTICE AND DELIBERATELY NOT AS A REQUIRED CI JOB. `gunbc compile --target dag --source-root <tree>/dag --source-root <tree>/src/v2 --entry <module>` on the plan file, priced as above (whole authority file rc 0 in 583 s; a broken copy refused in 49 s; a small sibling entry rc 0 in 44 s by the same session). Whether that route becomes a REQUIRED pre-check is the operator's call, because it adds CI wall time — royal-newt-240's own figure for the authority file is close to ten minutes per invocation — and naming it here is not a claim that CI should pay it. TWO LIMITS ON IT, stated so it is not over-read: `--target dag` emits 1 file where `--target rust` emits 43, so it is a parse-and-typecheck pre-check only and says nothing about Rust emission or about a refusal that emission's `compile_error!` arms carry; and it prices the FILE, which is not the same quantity as the cost of executing one label on an entry, which is what this class is about.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable, and the mitigation is real rather than decorative. The acquisition is bounded, so an unenterable leg returns a located rc (124) at a known second instead of running forever, and the interim pre-check above lets a lane or an author price an entry in minutes before anyone is asked to carry it. CEILING: mechanically preventable, NOT structural. Whether the entry a witness names has a closure whose measured cost exceeds the bound of the acquisition the lane will hold is a decidable question about two numbers the fleet can obtain — the entry's measured typecheck cost and the slot's bound — and a refusal can sit at the enrolment itself; nothing about the plan language makes that join impossible, and equally nothing about it makes the enrolment unwritable today, which is why the row sits below the ceiling rather than at it. The measured floor (~361 s per process, independent of entry size) is a separate quantity owned by `gunbc_run_pays_a_large_fixed_pre_entry_cost_per_invocation`; this row does not restate its cause, which that row keeps as an open reading.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER (a capability, not an artifact): the capability to price an ENTRY, once per entry at a pinned tree, and to admit an enrolment only against a bound the current bounded acquisition can carry. At that rung, naming a witness on an unreachable entry is refused where it is authored — the same fail-closed posture roadmap_node_identity_law takes for node identity in dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_model.dag:27, which is cited as the shape of the fix (make the invalid state unwritable at construction) and NOT as a precedent that it has landed here. Boundedness is part of the capability: a measured bound that no acquisition carries is exactly the number that makes the enrolment refuse.",

    "WHAT THIS ROW DOES NOT CLAIM. It does not claim that dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_authority.dag or dag/test/claim/roadmap/roadmap_authority.dag is too large, should be split, or is at fault — no measurement here prices an alternative entry, so any such change is a separate owner decision. It does not claim the 1860 s leg would never have finished: 1860 s is the cap, and the claim is bounded non-completion, not an estimate of the entry's total cost. It does not claim the named witnesses would fail, or that any specific plan row is wrong. It does not claim the fixed floor's mechanism (that cause belongs to the pre-entry-cost row). It does not claim the compile route should be required in CI. It does not claim manifest-matched provenance for any binary it names: both instruments here are recorded manifest-unmatched with their disk digests, and `release-bins.manifest` is not in the repo. And it does not claim a rung above mechanically preventable: no measurement here touches structurally guaranteed.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_execution_contract", decl_name: "ValidationOperation"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_execution_contract", decl_name: "WorkItemExecutionContract"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_claim_run_never_reaches_its_first_verdict_because_its_floor_is_a_whole_tree_closure", decl_name: "a_claim_run_never_reaches_its_first_verdict_because_its_floor_is_a_whole_tree_closure"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.gunbc_run_pays_a_large_fixed_pre_entry_cost_per_invocation", decl_name: "gunbc_run_pays_a_large_fixed_pre_entry_cost_per_invocation"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.witness_outside_gate_closure_falsified_by_other_file", decl_name: "witness_outside_gate_closure_falsified_by_other_file"),
  ],
}
